00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:54.540
<v Ira Glass>Act 3, "How did I get here?" So James Braly gave us the thoughts of somebody in a moment before infidelity occurs. Dani Shapiro has this story about the confusing mess it can be during the affair.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“我怎么会到这儿？”詹姆斯·布拉利给了我们出轨发生前瞬间的想法。丹妮·夏皮罗讲述了这个外遇期间混乱困境的故事。

00:35:54.540 --> 00:36:25.400
<v Dani Shapiro>DANI SHAPIRO: Here, in no particular order, are some things Lenny told me. That he and his wife didn't sleep in the same bed. That they haven't had a real marriage in years. That she was undergoing electroshock treatment in a clinic outside Philadelphia. That he had cancer and had to fly to Houston three days a week for chemotherapy. That his youngest daughter, age three, had a rare form of childhood leukemia. That he could not get a divorce for all of the above reasons. That he was heartbroken that he could not leave his wife and marry me.
<v Dani Shapiro>丹妮·夏皮罗：这里，顺序不分先后，是莱尼告诉我的一些事情。他和妻子不同床。他们已经多年没有真正的婚姻了。她在费城外的一家诊所接受电击治疗。他患有癌症，每周必须飞往休斯顿三次进行化疗。他最小的女儿，三岁，患有一种罕见的儿童白血病。由于上述所有原因，他无法离婚。他心碎于无法离开妻子并与我结婚。

00:36:25.400 --> 00:37:06.660
<v Dani Shapiro>For a long time I believed him, with every bone in my body I trusted that Lenny Klein was telling me the truth. When we talked about it, his jaw would tighten and his big brown eyes would fill with tears. His voice would quaver with pent-up, complex feelings that I couldn't possibly begin to understand. Poor Lenny. I marveled that so many bad things could happen to one person. And I vowed to take care of him. I exhorted myself to be a real woman, one who could step up to the plate and be good to her man in his moment of crisis.
<v Dani Shapiro>很长一段时间我都在相信他，我骨子里信任莱尼·克莱因说的是真话。当我们谈论这件事时，他的下巴会紧绷，他棕色的大眼睛会充满泪水。他的声音会因压抑的、复杂的我无法开始理解的感受而颤抖。可怜的莱尼。我惊叹于一个人怎么会发生这么多糟糕的事情。我发誓要照顾他。我告诫自己要成为一个真正的女人，一个能够在危机时刻挺身而出、善待男人的女人。

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:56.870
<v Dani Shapiro>Years later, I hold Lenny's lies up to the light and examine my own reasons for believing what, in retrospect, seems preposterous. I reread my old journals and noticed the way my girlish handwriting deteriorated into a scrawl as I wrote, "I have to be there for Lenny, he needs me, and he's going through so much. I don't know if I can handle it, but I have to be strong." I try to remember that Lenny was a trial lawyer, that he built an international reputation based on his own pathology. That he lied with an almost evangelical conviction. He prided himself on being able to convince anyone of anything.
<v Dani Shapiro>多年后，我把莱尼的谎言放在光下审视，并检查我自己相信那些回顾起来似乎荒谬的事情的原因。我重读了我的旧日记，注意到我少女时代的字迹在我写下时变得潦草：“我必须在莱尼身边，他需要我，他经历了这么多。我不知道我是否能应付，但我必须坚强。”我试着记住莱尼是一名辩护律师，他基于自己的病态建立了国际声誉。他以一种近乎福音派的信念撒谎。他以能说服任何人相信任何事情而自豪。

00:37:56.870 --> 00:38:28.070
<v Dani Shapiro>The lies had small beginnings. Lenny called me from a business trip and told me he was at Montreal airport, waiting to catch a flight to Calgary. I checked with the airline and found out that the flight would take approximately five hours, so when Lenny called an hour later to say he had landed in Calgary, I very calmly asked him where he really was. "Calgary," he said. "No, Lenny. Really." He stuck to his story. In the time that I knew him, he never, ever changed his story midstream.
<v Dani Shapiro>这些谎言都有小的开始。莱尼从出差途中给我打电话，告诉我他在蒙特利尔机场，等着赶往卡尔加里的航班。我查了一下航空公司，发现航班大约需要五个小时，所以当莱尼一小时后打电话说他已经在卡尔加里降落时，我非常平静地问他到底在哪里。“卡尔加里，”他说。“不，莱尼。真的。”他坚持他的说法。在我认识他的时间里，他从未，从未在中途改变过他的故事。

00:38:28.070 --> 00:38:59.350
<v Dani Shapiro>I hung up on him and called his family's house in Westchester. When the maid answered the phone, I asked to speak with Mr. Klein. And when he picked up the extension, and I heard his rough, craggy, "Hello," I screamed so hard into his ear that he dropped the receiver. He raced into the city, he let himself into my apartment and found me curled up in bed. He scooped me up and held me to his chest. His wife wasn't home, he told me. She was having shock treatment. And someone had to take care of his daughter.
<v Dani Shapiro>我挂断了他的电话，打给了他在威彻斯特的家。女佣接了电话，我要求和克莱因先生谈谈。当他接起分机，我听到他那粗哑的“喂”声时，我对着他的耳朵尖叫得太大声，以至于他扔掉了听筒。他冲进城里，溜进我的公寓，发现我蜷缩在床上。他把我抱起来，贴在胸口。他告诉我，他妻子不在家。她正在接受电击治疗。而必须有人照顾他的女儿。

00:38:59.350 --> 00:39:22.700
<v Dani Shapiro>He hadn't wanted to tell me because he wanted to spare me, to protect me from the horror of his life. Surely I understood. "Shush, sweetheart," he murmured into the top of my head as I wept, my face beet red like a little girl's. "So many people need me," he said. "But I love you best of all."
<v Dani Shapiro>他不想告诉我，因为他想保护我，让我免受他生活中恐怖的影响。肯定我理解。“嘘，亲爱的，”当我哭泣时，他对着我的头顶低语，我的脸像小女孩一样红得像甜菜。“很多人需要我，”他说。“但我最爱你。”

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:48.230
<v Dani Shapiro>Two years have passed, and something has gone wrong, terribly wrong with my life. I don't, in fact, think of my life as my life, but rather as a series of random events that have no logical connection. I'm no longer a student. I dropped out of Sarah Lawrence after my junior year, supposedly to pursue acting. And I'm actually doing a pretty good imitation of an actress. But I'm doing an even better imitation of a mistress.
<v Dani Shapiro>两年过去了，我的生活出了一些问题，出了严重的问题。事实上，我不再认为我的生活是我的生活，而是一系列没有任何逻辑联系的随机事件。我不再是学生了。大三后我从莎拉·劳伦斯学院辍学，据说为了追求表演。而且我实际上是在做一个相当好的演员模仿。但我做的更好的模仿是情妇。

00:39:48.230 --> 00:40:44.400
<v Dani Shapiro>Lenny's been busy buying me things. I don't particularly want these things, but they seem to be what Lenny is offering in lieu of himself. So quite suddenly, overnight really, I find myself driving a black Mercedes convertible. And just in case I might be mistaken for anything other than a kept woman, I wear a mink coat, a Cartier watch, a Bulgari necklace with an ancient coin at its center. The Mercedes is a step down from the first car Lenny gave me, when we had been going out for a month. A leased Ferrari. I don't know how to drive a stick shift, so the Ferrari was a bit of a problem. What I must have looked like, a 20-year-old blonde, dressed like Ivana Trump, stalled in traffic, grinding gears, trying to find the point on the clutch to hold that ridiculous car in place.
<v Dani Shapiro>莱尼一直忙着给我买东西。我并不特别想要这些东西，但它们似乎是莱尼代替自己提供的补偿。所以突然间，几乎一夜之间，我发现自己在开一辆黑色奔驰敞篷车。以防我被误认为是除了被包养的女人以外的任何人，我穿着水貂皮大衣，戴着卡地亚手表，戴着中心有一枚古钱币的宝格丽项链。奔驰比莱尼给我买的第一辆车低了一个档次，那是在我们约会一个月后。一辆租赁的法拉利。我不会开手动挡，所以法拉利有点麻烦。我看起来一定很滑稽，一个20岁的金发女郎，打扮得像伊万娜·特朗普，停在交通中，磨损齿轮，试图找到离合器的点来保持那辆荒谬的车位。

00:40:44.400 --> 00:41:14.010
<v Dani Shapiro>Lenny rented an apartment on a pretty little street in Greenwich Village. A furnished triplex with a garden, a fireplace, and a bedroom with a four-poster bed. He called it "our house," as if he didn't have another home with a whole family in it an hour north of the city. He kept half a dozen suits in the bedroom closet, and a brand new silk robe hung behind the bathroom door. There was an entire floor we didn't use, a large airy children's nursery.
<v Dani Shapiro>莱尼在格林威治村一条漂亮的小街上租了一套公寓。一套带有花园、壁炉和卧室里有一张四柱床的带家具的三层公寓。他称之为“我们的家”，好像他在城北一小时外没有另一个完整的家庭。他在卧室衣柜里放了半打西装，浴室门后挂着一件全新的丝绸长袍。有一整层楼我们没用，一间大的通风的儿童房。

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:37.640
<v Dani Shapiro>My parents knew that something was up. They knew I was going out with somebody, but they had no idea who. I was drifting away from them, and they were letting me go. One night I invited them over for dinner. I pushed all traces of Lenny out of sight, but, of course, there were clues. A glossy brochure for Italian yachts. A humidor in the center of the coffee table.
<v Dani Shapiro>我的父母知道出事了。他们知道我在和某人约会，但他们根本不知道是谁。我正在慢慢远离他们，而他们正任由我离去。一天晚上我邀请他们来吃晚饭。我把莱尼的所有痕迹都藏了起来，但当然，还是有线索。一本关于意大利游艇的精美宣传册。咖啡桌中心的一个雪茄盒。

00:41:37.640 --> 00:42:09.490
<v Dani Shapiro>I cooked up a storm and the place was filled with homey smells. Garlic, basil, coriander. It was winter, and the snow was piled up on the sills. Spotlights in the backyard shone on the landscaped garden, the redwood table, the Adirondack chairs. I had my father's favorite music, Dvorak's Symphony for the New World , playing on the stereo system.
<v Dani Shapiro>我做了一顿大餐，房间里充满了家的味道。大蒜、罗勒、香菜。是冬天，窗台上堆满了积雪。后院的聚光灯照在景观花园、红木桌和阿迪朗达克椅上。我播放了父亲最喜欢的音乐，德沃夏克的《新世界交响曲》。

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:33.890
<v Dani Shapiro>My parents rang the doorbell. They looked so solid standing on my front stoop, their cold, red noses poking out from above their mufflers. If nothing else, they looked like they belonged together. They were elegant and rangy, similarly proportioned. Unlike Lenny and me. Lenny's as thick as a linebacker, and I had become so delicate, the wind could have picked me up and blown me away.
<v Dani Shapiro>我父母按了门铃。站在前门廊上的他们看起来如此稳重，冰冷、红红的鼻子露在围巾上方。至少，他们看起来像是属于彼此的。他们优雅而高挑，比例匀称。不像我和莱尼。莱尼像线卫一样粗壮，而我变得如此纤细，风都能把我吹走。

00:42:33.890 --> 00:43:12.020
<v Dani Shapiro>My mother strode into the brownstone as if it wasn't the weirdest thing in the world to be visiting her daughter in a lavish apartment with no name on the outside buzzer. My father trailed behind her wearily, as if setting foot on another planet. My mother entered the living room, flung her arms wide, and did an impromptu dance to Dvorak. "Tra-la-la-la," she trilled. My father and I hung back and watched, our faces crumpled into awkward smiles. It didn't occur to me that she was frightened. That this was a lot for her to take in, her college dropout daughter living in the lap of luxury.
<v Dani Shapiro>我母亲大步走进棕色砂岩建筑，仿佛拜访她住在大公寓里且室外门铃上没名字的女儿并不是世界上最奇怪的事情。我父亲疲惫地跟在她后面，仿佛踏上了另一个星球。我母亲走进客厅，双臂张开，对着德沃夏克的音乐跳了一段即兴舞蹈。“啦-啦-啦-啦，”她轻哼道。父亲和我退缩着观看，我们的脸挤出尴尬的微笑。我没想到她很害怕。这让她接受起来很吃力，她辍学的女儿生活在奢侈的怀抱中。

00:43:12.020 --> 00:43:26.270
<v Dani Shapiro>All I could see was her outsized self, twirling around my living room in her fur coat and boots. I wanted a drink.
<v Dani Shapiro>我能看到的只有她超大的自我，穿着皮草大衣和靴子在我客厅里旋转。我想喝一杯。

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:52.210
<v Dani Shapiro>I poured two glasses of chardonnay for my parents and a large vodka for myself. I figured that if the vodka was in a water glass they wouldn't know the difference, especially if I drank it like it was water. My drinking had taken on a new urgency in the past few months. It was no longer a question of desire, but of need. I could not get through an evening like this without the armor of booze. I handed them their wine and directed them to the couch.
<v Dani Shapiro>我为父母倒了两杯霞多丽，为自己倒了一大杯伏特加。我想如果伏特加在水杯里他们不会知道区别，特别是如果我像喝水一样喝它。我的饮酒在过去几个月里有了新的紧迫感。不再是欲望的问题，而是需要。没有酒精的盔甲，我无法度过这样的夜晚。我把酒递给他们，指引他们坐到沙发上。

00:43:52.210 --> 00:44:19.970
<v Dani Shapiro>On the coffee table, I had put out a plate of crudites and a bowl of olives. "Quite a place," my mother said brightly, her gaze darting around the room at the white brick fireplace with its wrought iron tools, the glass wall overlooking the garden, the soaring ceiling. My father stared at the fringe of the rug, glassy eyed. He needed to be as numbed as I did to get through this night. "Thanks," I murmured, as if she was paying me a compliment.
<v Dani Shapiro>在咖啡桌上，我放了一盘生蔬菜和一碗橄榄。“真是个好地方，”我母亲明亮地说，目光在房间里打转，看着带有锻铁工具的白色砖壁炉、俯瞰花园的玻璃墙、高耸的天花板。我父亲呆滞地盯着地毯边缘。他也需要像我一样麻木才能度过这个夜晚。“谢谢，”我嘟囔着，仿佛她在夸奖我。

00:44:19.970 --> 00:44:52.390
<v Dani Shapiro>I checked on dinner, using the opportunity to gulp some wine from the open bottle in the fridge. Vodka and white wine was a combination I knew worked for me. If I stuck with the formula, things shouldn't be too bad in the morning. Especially if I wasn't eating, and I couldn't see myself eating. The music had stopped by the time we all sat at the dining room table, but I didn't notice then. If I had I would certainly have changed the tape, filled the air with something other than the tinny, lonely sound of our three forks scraping against plates.
<v Dani Shapiro>我去检查晚餐，借机喝了几口冰箱里打开的瓶子里的酒。伏特加和白葡萄酒是我知道对我有效的组合。如果我坚持这个公式，早上应该不会太糟糕。特别是如果我不吃东西，而且我也没胃口。当我们都坐在餐桌旁时，音乐已经停了，但我当时没注意到。如果我注意到了，我肯定会换磁带，让空气中充满除了我们三把叉子刮盘子的那种单薄、孤独的声音之外的东西。

00:44:52.390 --> 00:45:21.480
<v Dani Shapiro>I pushed my chicken from one side of my plate to the other. My stomach clenched and growled in protest. It seemed that my parents and I, after 22 years in each other's company, had run out of things to say. I already knew their views on the political situation in Israel, and we couldn't discuss my school work, I was no longer in school. My father pressed a corner of his napkin to his lips and murmured something about the food being delicious. My mother agreed.
<v Dani Shapiro>我把鸡肉从盘子的一边推到另一边。我的胃紧缩着，抗议着。似乎在陪伴了22年后，我和父母已经无话可说了。我已经知道他们对以色列政治局势的看法，而我们不能讨论我的学业，我已经不在学校了。父亲把餐巾的一角按在嘴唇上，嘟囔着说食物很美味。母亲表示同意。

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:59.640
<v Dani Shapiro>"My wonderful daughter," she said, shaking her head. "You've turned into such a little homemaker." I looked at my parents across the table. Is that what they really thought? How could they just sit there? Some small piece of me wanted my father to fling me over his shoulder and carry me kicking and screaming to the car he had parked outside. I secretly wished that they would drive me home, deposit me in my childhood bedroom, and feed me chicken soup and Saltines. I wanted to start my life over again, but I didn't know how.
<v Dani Shapiro>“我出色的女儿，”她说，摇着头。“你已经变成了一个小管家了。”我隔着桌子看着父母。他们真的是这么想的吗？他们怎么能就坐在那儿？我内心的一小部分希望父亲能把我扛在肩上，把我踢打尖叫着带到他停在外面的车里。我暗自希望他们能开车送我回家，把我安置在童年卧室里，喂我喝鸡汤和咸饼干。我想重新开始我的生活，但我不知道怎么做。

00:45:59.640 --> 00:46:23.480
<v Dani Shapiro>In the face of the most tangible proof that Lenny had been lying to me all these years, I remained with him. "My little girl is dying," he would say whenever I noticed the discrepancies in his stories. Or "My children's mother is having electroshock therapy." When I couldn't take my own confusion anymore-- Was Lenny lying to me? Was I going crazy?-- I decided to hire a detective to get to the bottom of it.
<v Dani Shapiro>面对莱尼这些年来一直对我撒谎的最实质证据，我仍然和他在一起。“我的小女孩快死了，”每当我注意到他故事里的出入时，他都会这样说。或者“我孩子的妈妈正在接受电击治疗。”当我再也无法承受自己的困惑时——莱尼在对我撒谎吗？我疯了吗？——我决定雇一名侦探来查个水落石出。

00:46:23.480 --> 00:46:56.300
<v Dani Shapiro>By this time, my parents knew all about me and Lenny in theory, but it wasn't something we could talk about. When I think back to my younger self rifling through the New York City yellow pages in search of a private investigator, I feel like I'm watching a movie about someone else. A girl so clueless she really didn't know that her desire to hire a detective was all the answer she needed. I chose a detective agency based on nothing more than its good address, in the East 60s, a neighborhood filled with private schools and shrinks.
<v Dani Shapiro>到这个时候，我父母在理论上对我和莱尼的事情都知道，但这并不是我们可以谈论的事情。当我回想起年轻时的自己翻阅纽约市黄页寻找私人侦探时，我觉得自己在看一部关于别人的电影。一个如此无知的女孩，她真的不知道她雇佣侦探的愿望就是她所需要的全部答案。我选择了一家侦探社，基于它良好的地址，在东60区，一个充满私立学校和心理医生的社区。

00:46:56.300 --> 00:47:44.490
<v Dani Shapiro>"This isn't what you think," I told the detective. "I'm in a relationship with a married man, and I want you to find out if my boyfriend is cheating on me with his wife." At this, his eyebrows shot up. "Come again?" "He claims his wife is in a mental hospital. He told me he hasn't been with her in years." "And you think he might be lying?" said the investigator. Did I see the laughter behind his eyes or is my memory supplying it now? Because I simply cannot imagine a middle-aged man listening to an earnest, over-dressed 22-year-old girl tell them that she thinks her boyfriend might still be sleeping with his wife. "Yes," I said.
<v Dani Shapiro>“这不是你想的那样，”我告诉侦探。“我和一个已婚男人有关系，我想让你查查我的男朋友是否在和他的妻子欺骗我。”听到这里，他挑起了眉毛。“再说一遍？”“他说他妻子在精神病院。他告诉我他们已经多年没有在一起了。”“你觉得他可能在撒谎吗？”调查员说。我看到了他眼中的笑意还是记忆现在加上去的？因为我简直无法想象一个中年男人听着一个认真、打扮过头的22岁女孩告诉他们她认为男朋友可能还在和妻子睡觉。“是的，”我说。

00:47:44.490 --> 00:48:01.360
<v Dani Shapiro>Days later, I got the proof about Lenny's lies. In tears, I called my mother. "Oh, darling. I'm so sorry. Is there anything I can do?" "I don't think so." A pause. "Do you want me to call his wife?"
<v Dani Shapiro>几天后，我得到了莱尼撒谎的证据。我含着泪给母亲打了电话。“哦，亲爱的。我很抱歉。我能做什么吗？”“我想不用。”停顿。“你要我给他妻子打电话吗？”

00:48:01.360 --> 00:48:14.270
<v Dani Shapiro>My mother and Mrs. Klein had met each other at a few school functions back when none of this could have struck anyone as a remote possibility. "Yes," I said. "Call her." "I'll do it right now," my mother said.
<v Dani Shapiro>我母亲和克莱因夫人在学校的一些活动上见过面，那时这一切还没让人觉得遥不可及。“是的，”我说。“打电话给她。”“我现在就打，”母亲说。

00:48:14.270 --> 00:48:40.640
<v Dani Shapiro>I sat by the phone and watched the minutes tick by. I pictured Lenny's wife answering the phone with a chirpy hello, and my mother's slow, steady explanation of why she was calling. I had set in motion a chain of events which was now unstoppable. More than 20 minutes passed before my mother called me back. "Well, I did it," she said. "You talked to her?" The world felt unreal, hallucinatory.
<v Dani Shapiro>我坐在电话旁，看着时间流逝。我想象着莱尼的妻子用轻快的声音接听电话，而我母亲稳重地解释为什么要打电话。我引发了一系列现在无法停止的事件。超过20分钟后，母亲才给我回电话。“好吧，我做了，”她说。“你跟她谈过了？”世界感觉不真实，有幻觉。

00:48:40.640 --> 00:49:08.950
<v Dani Shapiro>"Yes. She called me a liar. She told me she has a happy marriage to a man who travels a lot. That he's on his way to California. And I said, 'No, he's on his way to see my daughter.'" My mother sounded proud of herself, immersed in the drama of the moment. "How did she seem?" I asked. "What do you mean?" "Lenny's wife, was she angry?" "No," my mother said slowly. "She just didn't believe me, Dani."
<v Dani Shapiro>“是的。她骂我是个骗子。她告诉我她和一位经常出差的丈夫婚姻幸福。他在去加利福尼亚的路上。我说，‘不，他在去见我女儿的路上。’”我母亲听起来很自豪，沉浸在当下的戏剧性中。“她看起来怎么样？”我问。“你什么意思？”“莱尼的妻子，她生气了吗？”“没有，”我母亲慢慢地说。“她只是不相信我，丹妮。”

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:36.920
<v Dani Shapiro>I spent the rest of that day in a state of awful excitement. Something was going to happen. And when Lenny showed up that evening at the apartment we were still sharing in the West Village, I was ready. He put his bags down and gave me a hug. The phone rang. My mother had given Mrs. Klein the number at the apartment and suggested she find out for herself what her husband was up to. Lenny picked up the phone on the kitchen well. "Hello?"
<v Dani Shapiro>我在那一天的余下时间里处于一种糟糕的兴奋状态中。有什么事要发生了。当莱尼那天晚上出现在我们还在西村合住的公寓时，我准备好了。他放下包，拥抱了我。电话响了。我母亲给了克莱因夫人公寓的电话号码，并建议她自己查查她丈夫在搞什么鬼。莱尼接起了厨房壁上的电话。“喂？”

00:49:36.920 --> 00:50:02.060
<v Dani Shapiro>I watched him. And for the first and only time in the years I knew him, he looked genuinely surprised. He didn't say a word. He just listened for a few minutes, then hung up the phone. "That was my wife," he said. I was silent. "How did she get this number?" I shrugged. "I have to go." "I'd imagine," I said faintly.
<v Dani Shapiro>我看着他。在我认识他的那些年里，他第一次也是唯一一次看起来真的很惊讶。他一句话也没说。他只是听了几分钟，然后挂了电话。“那是我妻子，”他说。我沉默了。“她怎么得到这个号码的？”我耸耸肩。“我得走了。”“我想是这样，”我虚弱地说。

00:50:02.060 --> 00:50:34.170
<v Dani Shapiro>When Lenny slammed out of the apartment, I was certain I would never see him again. I knew the truth now. It was staring me in the face in the concrete form of flight lists and photos. And he knew that I knew. And besides, the whistle was blown. What could he possibly tell his wife? This was it, I told myself. Absolutely, positively the end.
<v Dani Shapiro>当莱尼猛地摔门离开公寓时，我确信我再也见不到他了。我现在知道了真相。它以飞行名单和照片的具体形式呈现在我面前。他知道我知道。而且，吹哨了。他还能对他妻子说什么呢？我对自己说，这就是一切。绝对的、肯定的终点。

00:50:34.170 --> 00:51:09.220
<v Dani Shapiro>It wasn't the end. Lenny still called 10, 12 times a day. He left messages on my answering machine. "Hello?" His voice filled my bedroom. "Fox, are you there?" Sometimes he didn't say a word. He would stay on the line for as long as five minutes, just breathing. Eventually he did get to me again. And for the next year that we were together, three days here, four days there, my life became unrecognizable to me. I idly wondered what it would take to get me to leave him.
<v Dani Shapiro>这不是终点。莱尼仍然每天打10、12次电话。他在我的留言机上留话。“喂？”他的声音充满了我的卧室。“狐狸，你在吗？”有时他一句话也不说。他会在电话线上待上五分钟，只是呼吸。最终他确实又联系上了我。在我们在一起的下一年里，这里三天，那里四天，我的生活对我来说变得无法辨认。我漫不经心地想，要花多少代价才能让我离开他。

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:52.750
<v Dani Shapiro>I wondered about this over bottles of chilled white wine, or heavy glasses half-filled with scotch. I was still wondering about it when I went to stay for a while at a health spa in California. The phone rang in my room one day. There had been a car crash on a snowy highway. My mother had 80 broken bones. My father was in a coma. They were lying in a hospital 3,000 miles away. And suddenly, in ways I could not have imagined seconds earlier, nothing else mattered. As I packed my bags, I remembered my mother twirling, dancing to Dvorak, through the doors of Lenny's brownstone. And the glassy look in my father's eyes.
<v Dani Shapiro>我在一瓶瓶冷冻的白葡萄酒，或半满威士忌的沉重玻璃杯中思考这个问题。当我搬到加利福尼亚的一家健康水疗中心住上一段时间时，我还在思考这个问题。一天我房间里的电话响了。雪地公路上发生了一起车祸。我母亲有80根骨头骨折。我父亲陷入昏迷。他们躺在3000英里外的医院里。突然之间，以我几秒前无法想象的方式，其他任何事情都无关紧要了。当我收拾行李时，我想起了母亲旋转着，伴随着德沃夏克的音乐，穿过莱尼棕色砂岩建筑大门。还有父亲眼中呆滞的神情。

00:51:52.750 --> 00:52:06.260
<v Dani Shapiro>I prayed that my father wouldn't die disappointed in me, and I knew then what I had to do.
<v Dani Shapiro>我祈祷父亲不会失望地死去，而我知道我那时必须做什么。

00:52:06.260 --> 00:52:23.740
<v Ira Glass>Dani Shapiro. That story is in her memoir, Slow Motion . She has another book, Devotion , coming out in February.
<v Ira Glass>丹妮·夏皮罗。那个故事在她的回忆录《慢动作》中。她还有另一本书《奉献》，二月份出版。

00:52:23.740 --> 00:52:44.960
<v Ira Glass>Act 4, "The Man Who Knew What I Was About to Say." So, in this second half of our program, we've had the moment before a possible affair, we've had what it's like during the affair, and now we turn to the aftermath of an affair. At least the very immediate aftermath. This story is by writer Etgar Keret, and read for us by actor Matt Malloy.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕，“知道我要说什么的男人。”所以，在我们节目的后半部分，我们经历了可能发生外遇前的时刻，经历了外遇期间的感受，现在我们转向外遇的后果。至少是非常直接的后果。这个故事由作家埃特加·凯雷特撰写，由演员马特·马洛伊为我们朗读。

00:52:44.960 --> 00:53:33.090
<v Matt Malloy>The man who knew what I was about to say sat next to me on the plane, a stupid smile plastered across his face. That's what's so nerve wracking about him. Smart, he wasn't. Or sensitive either. But still, he knew those lines and managed to say them. All the lines I meant to say, three seconds before me. "Do you sell Guerlain Mystique?" he asked the flight attendant, a minute before I could. And she gave him an orthodontic smile and said, "There's just one last bottle left." "My wife goes crazy for that perfume," he said. "She's positively addicted. If I come back from a trip without a bottle of Mystique from the duty free, she says I don't love her anymore. If I dare come into the house without at least one of these, I'm in deep [BLEEP]."
<v Matt Malloy>那个知道我要说什么的男人坐在我旁边的飞机上，脸上涂着愚蠢的微笑。这就是他最让我紧张的地方。他不聪明。也不敏感。但尽管如此，他知道那些台词，并设法说了出来。所有我打算在三秒前说的台词。“你卖娇兰秘境吗？”他问空姐，比我能问的时间早了一分钟。她给了他一个牙套微笑说：“只剩下最后一瓶了。”“我妻子对那种香水很着迷，”他说。“她简直上瘾了。如果我从旅行回来时没有带一瓶免税店的秘境，她说她就不再爱我了。如果我敢在没有至少带一瓶这种香水的情况下回家，我就死定了[哔——]。”

00:53:33.090 --> 00:54:01.030
<v Matt Malloy>That was supposed to be my line, but the man who knew what I was about to say stole it from me, without missing a beat. As soon as the wheels touched the ground, he switched on his mobile, a second before I did, and called his wife. "I just landed," he told her. "I'm sorry. I know it was supposed to be yesterday, flight was canceled. You don't believe me? Check it out yourself. Call Eric. I know you don't. I can give his number right now."
<v Matt Malloy>那本该是我的台词，但那个知道我要说什么的男人从我这里偷走了它，甚至没有停顿一下。轮子刚接触地面，他立刻打开了手机，比我早一秒，给他的妻子打电话。“我刚落地，”他告诉她。“对不起。我知道应该是昨天，航班被取消了。你不相信我？你自己查查。给埃里克打电话。我知道你不信。我现在就能给你他的电话。”

00:54:01.030 --> 00:54:12.310
<v Matt Malloy>I also have a travel agent called Eric. He'd lie for me, too.
<v Matt Malloy>我也有一个叫埃里克的旅行社代理人。他也会为我撒谎。

00:54:12.310 --> 00:54:39.230
<v Matt Malloy>When the plane reached the gate he was still talking on his mobile, giving all the answers I would have given, without a trace of emotion. Like a parrot in a world where time flows backwards, repeating whatever is about to be said instead of what's been said already. His answers were the best ones, under the circumstances. His circumstances weren't too hot, not too hot at all. Mine weren't either. Nobody was answering my call.
<v Matt Malloy>当飞机到达登机口时，他还在用手机通话，说着我本来会给出的所有答案，没有任何情感痕迹。就像一个时间倒流的世界里的鹦鹉，重复着即将说出的东西，而不是已经说过的。在那种情况下，他的回答是最好的。他的情况不太好，一点也不好。我的也不好。没有人接听我的电话。

00:54:39.230 --> 00:55:12.870
<v Matt Malloy>But just listening to the man who knew what I was about to say made me stop trying. Just listening to him, I could tell that this was a hole that even if I dug my way out of, it would be to a different reality. She'd never forgive me. She'd never trust me. Ever. All my coming trips would be hell on earth, and the time in between would be even worse. He went on talking and talking and talking. All those sentences that I'd thought up and hadn't said yet. It just kept flowing.
<v Matt Malloy>但仅仅听着那个知道我要说什么的男人说话，我就停止了尝试。仅仅听着他，我就能看出这是一个即便我拼命挖坑爬出去，也会通往不同现实的洞。她永远不会原谅我。她永远不会信任我。永远。我未来所有的旅行都会是人间地狱，而这期间的时间会更糟。他继续说着，说着，说着。所有那些我想出却还没说的句子。它就这样不断流出。

00:55:12.870 --> 00:55:37.910
<v Matt Malloy>He stepped it up, changing the intonation, like a drowning man struggling desperately to stay afloat. People began getting off. He got up, still talking, scooped up his laptop in the other hand and headed for the exit. I could see him forgetting it behind, the bag he had put in the overhead compartment. I could see him forgetting it. I didn't say anything. I just stayed put.
<v Matt Malloy>他加快了语速，改变了语调，就像一个垂死挣扎的人为了保持漂浮而拼命努力。人们开始下机。他站起来，还在说话，另一只手抓起笔记本电脑，朝着出口走去。我可以看到他把它忘在后面了，他放在行李架上的包。我可以看到他把它忘了。我什么也没说。我只是坐着不动。

00:55:37.910 --> 00:56:22.340
<v Matt Malloy>Gradually everyone walked out, till the only ones still there were an overweight religious woman with a million children and me. I got up and opened the overhead compartment above me, as if nothing. I took out the duty free bag like it had always been mine. Inside were the receipt and the bottle of Guerlain Mystique. My wife goes crazy for that perfume. She's positively addicted. If I come back from a trip without a bottle of Mystique from the duty free, she says I don't love her anymore.
<v Matt Malloy>渐渐地所有人都走了，直到唯一留在那里的是一个带着一百万个孩子的肥胖宗教女性和我。我站起来，像什么都没发生一样打开了上面的行李架。我拿出那袋免税商品，就好像它一直都是我的。里面是收据和那瓶娇兰秘境。我妻子对那种香水很着迷。她简直上瘾了。如果我从旅行回来时没有带一瓶免税店的秘境，她说她就不再爱我了。

00:56:22.340 --> 00:56:45.360
<v Ira Glass>Matt Malloy, reading a story by Etgar Keret, who is the author of several books of very, very short stories, most recently The Girl on the Fridge.
<v Ira Glass>马特·马洛伊，朗读了埃特加·凯雷特的故事，他是几本非常、非常短的故事集的作者，最近的一本是《冰箱上的女孩》。

